VALUES
(
  'How to Improve IELTS Listening from Band 6 to 7: A 4-Week Plan',
  'A focused four-week IELTS Listening plan that turns missed answers into a practical error log, targeted drills, and reliable timed-test habits.',
  'Improve IELTS Listening from Band 6 to 7 with a four-week error-log plan for focus, distractors, spelling, and realistic timed mock tests at home.',
  'https://www.ieltsai.pro/blog/improve-ielts-listening-band-6-to-7-plan',
  'blog',
  $post$
  <p>Moving from IELTS Listening Band 5.5 or 6 to Band 7 is rarely about finding one secret technique. The more useful question is: <strong>which answers are you losing, and why?</strong> A recent learner with one month left before a university deadline asked how to close exactly this score gap. Other candidates described strong everyday English but inconsistent Listening results under test pressure.</p>
  <p>The plan below replaces random practice with a four-week feedback loop. You will classify each error, drill the cause, and then prove the improvement in full tests.</p>

  <blockquote><p>Do not count only the wrong answers. Count the behaviour that produced them: lost focus, missed paraphrase, distractor, spelling, word-limit error, or a guess made too late.</p></blockquote>

  <h2>Start with the real score gap</h2>
  <p>The official IELTS scoring guide says Listening has 40 questions and each correct answer earns one mark. Raw marks are converted to whole and half bands. IELTS gives 23 correct answers as the average guide for Band 6 and 30 for Band 7, but the precise boundary can vary slightly between test versions.</p>
  <p>That means “I need Band 7” is not yet a practice target. Complete two official-style Listening tests under timed conditions and record the raw result by section and question type. If you score 25 and 27, your job is to recover several repeatable marks—not to rebuild all of your English in four weeks.</p>

  <h2>Build a Listening error log</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Error code</th><th scope="col">What happened</th><th scope="col">Best next drill</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>F</td><td>You lost focus and missed where the recording moved.</td><td>Replay from 20 seconds earlier and mark the phrase that signalled the next answer.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>P</td><td>You heard the idea but missed its paraphrase.</td><td>Write the question phrase beside the different phrase used in the audio.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>D</td><td>You chose an option that was mentioned, then rejected or changed.</td><td>Underline contrast signals such as <em>but</em>, <em>however</em>, and <em>actually</em>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>S</td><td>Your meaning was correct but spelling, plural, or grammar was wrong.</td><td>Dictate the answer sentence and check the exact required form.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>I</td><td>You misread the instruction or word limit.</td><td>Rewrite the instruction as a short rule before replaying the item.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>R</td><td>You were still thinking about one answer when the recording advanced.</td><td>Practise a five-second stop-loss: guess, mark it, and rejoin immediately.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>After each test, add one code beside every missed item. At the end of the week, total the codes. Your largest category decides the next drill. This prevents you from spending hours on vocabulary when the real problem is recovery, or repeating full mocks when five marks disappear through spelling.</p>

  <h2>Week 1: diagnose and repair attention</h2>
  <p>Complete two sections at a time rather than a full test every day. Before listening, preview the questions and identify what each blank or option asks for. During review, replay only the evidence zone, then check the transcript. Find the exact words that moved the conversation from the tempting answer to the correct one.</p>
  <p>IELTS states that the Listening recording is heard once and that answers follow the order of information in the recording. Train yourself to notice forward movement. If one answer disappears, your priority is the next question—not a perfect reconstruction of the previous sentence.</p>

  <h2>Week 2: target your two largest error types</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>For distractors:</strong> compare the options before the audio and circle the one or two words that make each meaning different.</li>
    <li><strong>For paraphrase:</strong> build pairs from real mistakes, such as “postpone” and “move to a later date.” Review meanings, not isolated synonyms.</li>
    <li><strong>For spelling and form:</strong> run five-minute dictations and check articles, plurals, numbers, dates, and compound words.</li>
    <li><strong>For lost focus:</strong> practise short sections without pausing, then write a one-line summary of what each speaker decided.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If map questions are one of your weak areas, use the <a href="/blog/ielts-listening-map-labelling-strategy">predict-track-answer map strategy</a> rather than treating every Listening task the same way.</p>

  <h2>Week 3: combine accuracy with test pace</h2>
  <p>Return to full sections and then full tests. Use headphones and the same answer-entry method you expect on test day. Do not pause, change the speed, or check the transcript until the section ends. After review, repeat only the missed sequence once without stopping. The goal is to carry a corrected behaviour into normal pace.</p>
  <aside><p><strong>Decision rule:</strong> if full-test scores are not improving but one error code is shrinking, keep the targeted drill for three more sessions. If the same code still dominates, change the drill—not the target.</p></aside>

  <h2>Week 4: prove consistency and taper</h2>
  <p>Complete three realistic tests on separate days. Record the raw score, error-code totals, and the point at which concentration dropped. Your readiness signal is not one lucky Band 7 estimate. It is a stable range with no single error pattern repeatedly costing several answers.</p>
  <p>In the final two days, review the error log and do one or two short sections. Avoid exhausting yourself with repeated full tests. Check your centre instructions, equipment expectations, and test-day timing.</p>

  <h2>Your four-week checklist</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>I have two baseline scores and know my largest error categories.</li>
    <li>I can identify the audio evidence for every corrected answer.</li>
    <li>I recover quickly instead of carrying one missed answer into the next.</li>
    <li>I check word limits, spelling, and singular or plural form.</li>
    <li>My last three realistic tests show a stable range.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Final action: run a diagnostic today</h2>
  <p>Start one timed test in the <a href="/listening">IELTS Listening practice area</a> or choose a complete set from <a href="/cambridge-tests">Cambridge IELTS practice</a>. Create the six-code error log before you check the answers. If screen workflow also costs you marks, pair this plan with the <a href="/blog/ielts-computer-test-typing-screen-strategy">7-day computer-test guide</a>.</p>
  <p>Official facts in this guide were checked on 19 July 2026 against the IELTS <a href="https://ielts.org/take-a-test/your-results/ielts-scoring-in-detail" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">scoring explanation</a> and <a href="https://ielts.org/organisations/ielts-for-organisations/test-types/ielts-academic-test/academic-test-format-in-detail" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">test-format details</a>.</p>
  $post$,
  'improve-ielts-listening-band-6-to-7-plan',
  ARRAY['improve IELTS Listening score', 'IELTS Listening Band 6 to 7', 'IELTS Listening study plan', 'IELTS Listening error log', 'IELTS Listening practice', 'IELTS Listening tips'],
  'Listening', 5, '2026-07-19T08:00:00Z'::timestamptz, false, false, 'free',
