(
  'IELTS Speaking Band 5 vs 6 vs 7: What Changes?',
  'Compare how IELTS Speaking performance develops from Band 5 to 6 to 7, then use a practical Part 3 framework to improve your recorded answers.',
  'Compare IELTS Speaking Bands 5, 6, and 7 across fluency, vocabulary, grammar, and pronunciation, then practise stronger Part 3 answers with confidence.',
  'https://www.ieltsai.pro/blog/ielts-speaking-band-5-vs-6-vs-7',
  'blog',
  $post$
  <p>Learners often ask for Band 5, 6, and 7 IELTS Speaking samples because a single “good answer” does not show what actually changes between levels. A recent candidate specifically wanted recordings or transcripts that reveal those differences. Others felt their Part 3 answers were too short or difficult to develop, even when their everyday English felt natural.</p>
  <p>The important point is that IELTS does not award a band from one memorised answer. Examiners assess your performance across the test using four equally weighted criteria: fluency and coherence, lexical resource, grammatical range and accuracy, and pronunciation.</p>

  <blockquote><p>The move from Band 5 to 7 is not “use fancier words.” It is greater control: ideas are easier to follow, vocabulary is more flexible, complex sentences work more reliably, and pronunciation supports clear communication.</p></blockquote>

  <h2>The four official criteria</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Criterion</th><th scope="col">What to listen for in your recording</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Fluency and coherence</td><td>Can you keep going, connect ideas logically, and avoid long content-search pauses?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Lexical resource</td><td>Can you choose precise everyday words, paraphrase, and discuss unfamiliar topics without collapsing?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Grammatical range and accuracy</td><td>Can you mix simple and complex structures while keeping enough sentences error-free?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Pronunciation</td><td>Can a listener follow you without strain, with useful stress, rhythm, and intonation?</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Accent is not a fifth criterion. The goal is intelligibility and control, not copying a British or American voice.</p>

  <h2>Band 5 vs 6 vs 7 in plain English</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Feature</th><th scope="col">Around Band 5</th><th scope="col">Around Band 6</th><th scope="col">Around Band 7</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Flow</td><td>Simple ideas continue, but complex answers bring repetition, slow speech, or noticeable searching.</td><td>You can speak at length, though hesitation or self-correction sometimes weakens coherence.</td><td>You produce longer turns readily; some hesitation remains, but it does not normally damage the message.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Vocabulary</td><td>Vocabulary works for familiar topics but has limited flexibility; paraphrase is uneven.</td><td>You have enough range to discuss topics at length and usually paraphrase successfully.</td><td>You use vocabulary flexibly across topics, including some less common or idiomatic items with awareness of style.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Grammar</td><td>Basic forms are reasonably controlled, while complex structures are limited and error-prone.</td><td>You use both simple and complex forms, but errors in complex sentences are frequent.</td><td>You use a range of complex structures with flexibility, and many sentences are error-free.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Pronunciation</td><td>Speech is generally understandable, but mispronunciation can make the listener work.</td><td>Pronunciation features are used with mixed control; meaning is generally clear.</td><td>You use a wider range of pronunciation features with enough control to stay easy to understand.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>These are practical paraphrases of the public band descriptors, not a replacement for them. A candidate does not need every Band 7 feature in every sentence; the official descriptor explains that the score reflects the overall fit across positive features.</p>

  <h2>One Part 3 question at three levels</h2>
  <p>Imagine the examiner asks: <em>Why do some people prefer working from home?</em></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Developing response:</strong> “Because it is comfortable. People do not travel, and they have more time.” The answer is relevant but stops before explaining the consequence.</li>
    <li><strong>More controlled response:</strong> “One reason is that people can avoid a long commute, so they have more time and energy for work. For example, someone who normally travels an hour each way can use part of that time to plan the day.” The idea, reason, and example connect clearly.</li>
    <li><strong>Flexible response:</strong> “Avoiding the commute is a major attraction, particularly in large cities. It can improve concentration because employees begin the day with more energy; however, that benefit depends on having a quiet space at home.” The response develops, qualifies, and contrasts without sounding rehearsed.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The third response is not stronger because it is longer. It gives the claim a reason, consequence, and sensible limit.</p>

  <h2>Use the A-R-E-C framework for Part 3</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Answer:</strong> give your position directly.</li>
    <li><strong>Reason:</strong> explain why it is true.</li>
    <li><strong>Example:</strong> make the reasoning concrete.</li>
    <li><strong>Contrast:</strong> when useful, show another group, time, place, or limitation.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>You do not need all four moves every time. Use Answer + Reason for a simple question; add Example or Contrast when the examiner invites deeper analysis. This keeps the structure flexible and conversational.</p>

  <h2>If you cannot think of an idea</h2>
  <p>Part 3 asks you to discuss issues in a general and more abstract way. It tests your ability to explain opinions, analyse, discuss, and speculate—not your expertise in the topic. Choose a reasonable position you can explain. You may compare older and younger people, cities and rural areas, past and present, individuals and governments, or short-term and long-term effects.</p>
  <aside><p><strong>Useful self-check:</strong> after recording, underline the exact sentence that answers the question. If you cannot find it, improve directness before adding advanced vocabulary.</p></aside>

  <h2>A 15-minute Band 6-to-7 drill</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Record three Part 3 answers without restarting.</li>
    <li>Transcribe one answer exactly, including repetitions and unfinished sentences.</li>
    <li>Mark A, R, E, and C beside the moves you actually used.</li>
    <li>Choose one change only: clearer position, stronger reason, useful example, or cleaner complex sentence.</li>
    <li>Record the answer again, then compare clarity rather than word count.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Final action: record one Part 3 set</h2>
  <p>Open the <a href="/speaking">IELTS Speaking practice area</a> and complete one set without memorising a script. Review it against all four criteria, then repeat only the weakest answer using A-R-E-C. If your Part 2 answers also end early, use the <a href="/blog/ielts-speaking-part-2-two-minute-answer">two-minute Part 2 framework</a>. You can explore how skill scores affect a target overall result with the <a href="/ielts-band-score-calculator">IELTS band score calculator</a>.</p>
  <p>Official claims were checked on 19 July 2026 against the IELTS <a href="https://ielts.org/take-a-test/test-types/ielts-academic-test/ielts-academic-format-speaking" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Speaking test format</a> and current <a href="https://ielts.org/cdn/ielts-guides/ielts-speaking-band-descriptors.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Speaking band descriptors</a>.</p>
